# DATA SOVEREIGNTY: Infinity Protocol v8.4.0

## PREAMBLE — THE ROOT CAUSE OF ALL SEEDING & SECURITY FAILURES

Every seeding and security failure across the project fleet stems from one or more of these **ten sins**:

1. **The Client SDK Sin** — Using `firebase/firestore` (browser SDK) in a Node.js seed script. It has no Admin permissions. ALWAYS use the **Admin SDK**.
2. **The Hardcoded Project ID Sin** — `admin.initializeApp({ projectId: 'some-project' })` will silently seed the **wrong project** in CI. ALWAYS use `applicationDefault()`.
3. **The Missing Validation Sin** — Writing raw, unvalidated JavaScript objects to Firestore. Schema drift is inevitable. **Zod schema MUST be a gatekeeper**.
4. **The Open Endpoint Sin** — An HTTP-triggered Cloud Function seed endpoint with no auth guard. This is a **zero-day attack surface**. ALL HTTP seed endpoints MUST verify a `SEED_SECRET`.
5. **The Re-run Data Drift Sin** — Seed scripts that run on every deploy without idempotency tracking. Documents accumulate orphaned data. ALWAYS write a `_seedMeta` version checkpoint and check it first.
6. **The Stripe Desync Sin** — Price IDs or amounts in Firestore diverge from the Stripe Product/Price objects. The payment engine then charges the wrong amount or fails. Firestore is the **server-side source of truth**; Stripe metadata must be pinned to it.
7. **The CI Blind-Spot Sin** — Product seeding is never verified in CI. A broken catalog ships to production undetected. CI MUST verify that `_seedMeta` documents exist and are at the expected version.
8. **The Sequential Write Sin** — Calling `await db.collection().doc().set()` in a `for` loop (one network round-trip per document). For N > 10, this is catastrophically slow. ALWAYS use `batch.set()` or `BulkWriter`.
9. **The Local Production Sin** — Developers run seed scripts against the live production Firestore because no emulator is configured. This corrupts production data. ALWAYS run `dv seed` against the emulator first.
10. **The Rules Drift Sin** — The local `firestore.rules` file is LESS SECURE than the deployed rules. Running `firebase deploy --only firestore:rules` overwrites production with the weaker local version, silently downgrading your security posture. **`dv rules:status` MUST be run before every deploy.**


