## LAW 5: HTTP SEED ENDPOINT SECURITY

### The Rule
- Cloud Function seed endpoints (`onRequest`) MUST be protected by a `SEED_SECRET` from GCP Secret Manager.
- They MUST be removed or disabled (by removing the export) after the initial seeding is confirmed.
- NEVER leave a seed endpoint permanently deployed — it is a write-access back door.

### Anti-Pattern (BANNED)
```typescript
// ❌ BANNED — commented-out auth guard
export const seedProducts = onRequest({ cors: true }, async (req, res) => {
    // if (req.query.secret !== "my-secret") { // <--- COMMENTED OUT = ZERO-DAY
    //     res.status(403).send("Unauthorized");
    //     return;
    // }
    // ... writes to Firestore
});
```

### Canonical Pattern (REQUIRED)
```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT — secret validated from environment
import { defineSecret } from 'firebase-functions/params';
const seedSecret = defineSecret('SEED_SECRET');

export const seedProducts = onRequest({ cors: false, secrets: [seedSecret] }, async (req, res) => {
    const providedSecret = req.headers['x-seed-secret'];
    if (!providedSecret || providedSecret !== seedSecret.value()) {
        res.status(403).json({ error: 'Unauthorized' });
        return;
    }
    // ... safe to proceed
});
```

