## LAW 9: CI SEED VERIFICATION GATE

### The Rule
- EVERY CI pipeline MUST include a `Seed Verification` step as part of the deployment gate.
- The step verifies that `_seedMeta/products` exists in Firestore and its `version` matches the expected `SEED_VERSION` constant.
- If verification fails, the CI pipeline MUST fail with a non-zero exit code — the deployment MUST NOT proceed.
- Use the Firebase Admin SDK with `GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS` set to a CI service account secret.

### CI Step (REQUIRED — add to `templates/ci-test.yml`)
```yaml
- name: Verify Firestore Seed Integrity (Data Sovereignty v8.4.0)
  env:
    GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS: ${{ secrets.GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS_JSON_PATH }}
  run: |
    node -e "
    const admin = require('firebase-admin');
    admin.initializeApp({ credential: admin.credential.applicationDefault() });
    const db = admin.firestore();
    const EXPECTED_VERSION = require('./scripts/seed-products').SEED_VERSION || 'v1.0.0';
    db.collection('_seedMeta').doc('products').get().then(snap => {
      if (!snap.exists) { console.error('[SEED GATE] _seedMeta/products missing!'); process.exit(1); }
      const v = snap.data()?.version;
      if (v !== EXPECTED_VERSION) { console.error('[SEED GATE] Version mismatch:', v, '!=', EXPECTED_VERSION); process.exit(1); }
      console.log('[SEED GATE] ✅ Firestore catalog verified at version', v);
      process.exit(0);
    }).catch(e => { console.error(e); process.exit(1); });
    "
```

